{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED on exact clean pushed head 43daa5f03f3d662b7ab2ce83c63c05d753eb1aeb and the co-signed ui and internal/service artifacts.\n\nThe first three availability blockers from 20978478 are resolved. App constructs one memoized RecordIndex and passes it to Thread; reverse-provenance buckets use get-or-create plus push; and Thread, ElidedRecord, and semantic-action lookup now use index statement, act, decision, and commitment reads rather than projection scans. The numeric limit is pinned: changing REFERENCE_LIMIT from 12 to 1000000 makes the focused many-reference test fail.\n\nTwo issues remain.\n\n1. The required explicit expansion is still untested. The earlier verdict required a fixture proving both the bounded initial twelve and that explicit expansion reveals the rest. The new test proves only the initial SSR markup and remainder sentence. In a detached exact-head mutation worktree I changed the show-all button onClick to undefined; all 71 UI tests still passed. Add a DOM interaction test with more than twelve references that clicks the button, observes all thirty in order, and fails when the handler is disabled.\n\n2. The new proposal-ratification closing station can name a retired ratification as current. buildSpine uses projection.acts.find for the first effective ratify act. The fold contract in activeRatification is deliberately different: a statement may be ratified more than once after retirement and restoration, and the current answer is the latest surviving effective ratification. Projected acts retain their original effective verdict after an act is retired. A proposal with ratify A, supersede A, then ratify B is therefore rendered as closed by A, even though B is the live ratification. Select the latest surviving ratification by the same semantics as the fold, or project the current ratification explicitly, and add a regression that distinguishes A from B. Do not reproduce authority semantics approximately in the UI.\n\nArchitecture: this head changes the layer-7 UI surface and internal/service embedded distribution. It preserves kernel, fold, projection, and service-route contracts; no architecture page update is needed. The first defect is a missing availability regression. The second is an incorrect UI interpretation of existing fold authority, not a contract change.\n\nSecurity: projection values remain React-escaped; no credential, signer, mutation route, or secret handling changes. The initial untrusted fan-out bound is real and mutation-sensitive. However, the untested expansion interaction weakens availability assurance, and showing a retired ratification as the current closer is an authority/integrity display defect. Both must be resolved before approval.\n\nSimplification: keep the single shared index and O(n) bucket construction. Add one direct interaction regression for References and one shared active-ratification selector whose ordering and retirement rule visibly match the fold; avoid another local first-match approximation.\n\nIndependent gates on the exact head pass: npm test 71/71; make ui-check including TypeScript and regenerated embed parity; go test ./internal/service/... ./internal/wireparity/...; git diff --check. npm ci reports one existing high-severity dependency advisory, but this head changes no package manifest or lockfile. This verdict applies only to 43daa5f0; any repair requires new exact-head artifacts and review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:4eddb211dfa8dfc2479042837aa0159c1d48607e","head":"43daa5f03f3d662b7ab2ce83c63c05d753eb1aeb","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:3ccdd5dcbe84115366db3025186394278522d2b8, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6fdedb1c5b5da5578078dd3268503c23ce564b20","verdict":"changes-requested"}}